{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Them and Pinchas.</b> Rashi explained that he went to avenge the vengeance of Joseph his maternal grandfather, etc. (Sotah 43a) — meaning that Moses was confident in him, that he would not spare them and would not leave them root or branch. For otherwise Pinchas would have hated them on account of the recognition that they had sold Joseph into Egypt (Genesis 37:36) — a place set aside for harlotry — and now the Midianites had seized their fathers' deed in their own hands to cause Israel to stumble through harlotry. And what he explained — that because he began the mitzvah, he should finish it — means that a sign was given to him: just as you began to bring them down, for you brought down Cozbi the daughter of a king, so shall they all fall before you. And without doubt he had a courageous heart against them more than Elazar did, since they had already fallen before him and been delivered into his hand; and because he had begun the mitzvah, it was incumbent upon him to bring the matter to completion, for if a mitzvah draws after it a mitzvah, then how much more does a part of it draw the whole of it. And Moses did not wish to go because he was raised in Midian, and he feared that if the men of war were to be negligent in any matter, they would pin the shortfall on Moses, and they would say that he had spared the place where he was raised." "And as for what it says, 'a thousand per tribe' — the commentators said that twelve thousand is one part in fifty of six hundred thousand; therefore they brought the spoil, one portion out of fifty, like the standard rate of terumah of the median kind. And from the fact that it says 'a thousand per tribe' twice, the Tanhuma (Matot 3) deduces that he took two thousand from each tribe. And the reason it does not say 'two thousand per tribe' is that only a thousand per tribe went out to the battle, and a thousand sat over the vessels; and between them all, twenty-four thousand — to avenge upon them what they had caused to fall of twenty-four thousand of Israel." "And as for the fact that it wrote 'a thousand לַמַּטֶּה' (per tribe) and did not say 'a thousand מִמַּטֶּה' (from a tribe)' — it is because the merit of the tribe itself also went with them to battle, and the thousand was joined to the tribe itself, for its merit assisted them; therefore they gave from the spoil also to the entire congregation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:33.986272646Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}